<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ram Mohan Roy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Raja Ram Mohan Roy, Rammohun Roy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ram Mohan Roy was a Bengali scholar and social reformer, often called the father of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. He campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a>, the burning of widows, which the British banned in Bengal in 1829, and argued for women's property rights and modern education. In 1828 he founded the Brahmo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabha-libya">Sabha</a>, a monotheistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> reform society. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> while visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy.jpg" alt="Ram Mohan Roy" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rembrandt Peale · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Raja_Ram_Mohun_Roy,_1833.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ram Mohan Roy was born on 22 May 1772 at Radhanagar in Hooghly District, in the Bengal Presidency. His great-grandfather was a Rarhi Kulin Brahmin; in the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulinism">Kulinism</a> was a synonym for polygamy and the dowry system, both of which he later campaigned against. He became a scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Persian and English, and also knew Arabic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek. In his childhood he witnessed the death of his sister-in-law by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a>; he tried to protest, but she was burned alive.</p><p>From 1803 to 1815 he worked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s Writing Service, first as private clerk at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> and then with the collector John Digby. In 1804 he wrote Tuhfat-ul-Muwahhidin, A Gift to Monotheists, in Persian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1814 he started the Atmiya Sabha, a discussion circle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> that propagated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> and campaigned against idolatry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> rigidity and meaningless ritual. He wrote against Trinitarian Christianity and published The Precepts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in 1820. He founded the Anglo-Hindu School in 1822 and the Vedanta College in 1826. His Bengali weekly Sambad Kaumudi, begun in 1821, covered freedom of the press and the admission of Indians to high office. His Gaudiya Vyakaran, published in 1826, was the first complete Bengali grammar.</p><p>In 1828 he founded the Brahmo Sabha with Debendranath Tagore, a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmo-samaj">Brahmo Samaj</a>, whose beliefs rested on the worship of one God and the equality of all people. He attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a>, polygamy, child marriage and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, and he petitioned against sati in 1829. In 1830 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as an envoy of the Mughal emperor Akbar II, who had given him the title of Raja, to make sure that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-william-bentinck">Lord William Bentinck</a>'s Bengal Sati Regulation of 1829 was not overturned and to seek a larger stipend for the emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In early September 1833 Roy came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> to visit his Unitarian friend Lant Carpenter. He fell ill in mid-September and died at Stapleton on 27 September 1833 of meningitis or a chronic respiratory ailment. He was first buried at Stapleton Grove and in 1843 reburied at Arnos Vale Cemetery, where a chhatri designed by William Prinsep stands over the tomb.</p><p>Roy tried to join Western culture with the best of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s own traditions. His commitment to English education later prompted a debate between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, who objected to it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>, who answered that Roy had the full inheritance of Indian wisdom and was never a schoolboy of the West.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raja Ram Mohan Roy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ram Mohan Roy</b> founded the Brahmo Samaj in 1828 to promote a rational, monotheistic Hinduism; Dayananda Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj in 1875 to return to the Vedas; and Swami Vivekananda’s address to the World’s Parliament of Religions in Chicago in 1893 introduced Hindu philosophy to Western audiences.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The same decades saw movements of religious and social reform, from the Brahmo Samaj, founded by <b>Ram Mohan Roy</b> in 1828, to the Arya Samaj of Dayananda Saraswati, founded in 1875, which called Hindus back to the Vedas.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Colebrooke made the first Sanskrit-to-English translation, of the Aitareya, in 1805, and <b>Rammohun Roy</b> the first English Kena in 1816.</blockquote><a href="/work/upanishads">Upanishads · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1827 <b>Ram Mohan Roy</b> published a dissertation on the mantra; from 1830 members of the Brahmo Samaj used it in private devotion, and the First Covenant of 1843 required it for worship.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gayatri-mantra">Gayatri Mantra</a></li><li><blockquote>Calcutta became the commercial and intellectual capital of British India, the centre of the Bengal Renaissance associated with <b>Ram Mohan Roy</b> and Rabindranath Tagore.</blockquote><a href="/state/bengal-presidency">Bengal Presidency</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Radhanagore, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.73,87.87&amp;year=1811" aria-label="Radhanagore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6900 1325 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ram Mohan Roy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7350 1595h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7350 1595h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.73,87.87&amp;year=1811">What was here in 1811? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Humbly reluctant as the natives of India are to obtrude upon the notice of government the sentiments they entertain on any public measure, there are circumstances when silence would be carrying this respectful feeling to culpable excess.&#8221;</p><footer>A Letter on English Education, to Lord Amherst, 1823 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_English_Works_of_Raja_Rammohun_Roy/Volume_II/A_Letter_on_English_Education" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the same manner the Sanskrit system of education would be the best calculated to keep this country in darkness, if such had been the policy of the British legislature.&#8221;</p><footer>A Letter on English Education, to Lord Amherst, 1823 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_English_Works_of_Raja_Rammohun_Roy/Volume_II/A_Letter_on_English_Education" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
